  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "The expression\n\n\\[ \\frac{P+Q}{P-Q}-\\frac{P-Q}{P+Q}, \\]\n\nwhere \\(P=x+y\\) and \\(Q=x-y,\\) is equivalent to:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x^2-y^2}{xy}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x^2-y^2}{2xy}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{xy}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{2xy}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "a",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1964-p4.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since \\(P+Q=2x\\) and \\(P-Q=2y,\\) the expression becomes \\[\\frac{x}{y}-\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{x^2-y^2}{xy}.\\]\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A.",
      "hints": [
        "First substitute \\(P+Q=2x\\) and \\(P-Q=2y\\)",
        "Simplify \\(\\frac{x}{y}-\\frac{y}{x}\\) using a common denominator"
      ],
